<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>Simplify Expressions Using the Distributive Property: Mini-Lesson Review</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01545</md:content-id>
    <md:title>Simplify Expressions Using the Distributive Property: Mini-Lesson Review</md:title>
    <md:uuid>5b8ca2e6-d0f0-4739-b59d-fe07de52ca84</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">  <title>Question #2: Simplify Expressions Using the Distributive Property</title>
      <para id="para-00001">Which expression is equivalent to \(-2(x-4)\)?</para>
  </section>
    <section id="sect-00002">
      <title>Simplify Expressions Using the Distributive Property</title>
      <para id="para-00002">The Distributive Property can be used to remove parentheses as you simplify expressions.</para>
      <note id="note-00001">
        <para id="para-00003">
         <emphasis> DISTRIBUTIVE PROPERTY</emphasis> 
      </para>
        
        <para id="para-00004">
          <m:math display="block">
            <m:semantics>
              <m:mrow>
                <m:mrow>
                  <m:mtable>
                    <m:mtr>
                      <m:mtd columnalign="left">
                        <m:mtext>If</m:mtext>
                        <m:mspace width="0.2em"/>
                        <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                        <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                        <m:mi>b</m:mi>
                        <m:mo>,</m:mo>
                        <m:mi>c</m:mi>
                        <m:mspace width="0.2em"/>
                        <m:mtext>are real numbers, then</m:mtext>
                      </m:mtd>
                      <m:mtd/>
                      <m:mtd/>
                      <m:mtd columnalign="left">
                        <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                        <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                        <m:mi>b</m:mi>
                        <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                        <m:mi>c</m:mi>
                        <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                        <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                        <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                        <m:mi>b</m:mi>
                        <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                        <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                        <m:mi>c</m:mi>
                      </m:mtd>
                    </m:mtr>
                    <m:mtr/>
                    <m:mtr/>
                    <m:mtr>
                      <m:mtd columnalign="right">
                        <m:mtext>Also,</m:mtext>
                      </m:mtd>
                      <m:mtd/>
                      <m:mtd/>
                      <m:mtd columnalign="left">
                        <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                        <m:mi>b</m:mi>
                        <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                        <m:mi>c</m:mi>
                        <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                        <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                        <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                        <m:mi>b</m:mi>
                        <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                        <m:mo>+</m:mo>
                        <m:mi>c</m:mi>
                        <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                      </m:mtd>
                    </m:mtr>
                    <m:mtr>
                      <m:mtd/>
                      <m:mtd/>
                      <m:mtd/>
                      <m:mtd columnalign="left">
                        <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                        <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                        <m:mi>b</m:mi>
                        <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                        <m:mi>c</m:mi>
                        <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                        <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                        <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                        <m:mi>b</m:mi>
                        <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                        <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                        <m:mi>c</m:mi>
                      </m:mtd>
                    </m:mtr>
                    <m:mtr>
                      <m:mtd/>
                      <m:mtd/>
                      <m:mtd/>
                      <m:mtd columnalign="left">
                        <m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo>
                        <m:mi>b</m:mi>
                        <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                        <m:mi>c</m:mi>
                        <m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo>
                        <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                        <m:mo>=</m:mo>
                        <m:mi>b</m:mi>
                        <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                        <m:mo>−</m:mo>
                        <m:mi>c</m:mi>
                        <m:mi>a</m:mi>
                      </m:mtd>
                    </m:mtr>
                  </m:mtable>
                </m:mrow>
              </m:mrow>
            </m:semantics>
          </m:math>
      </para>
      </note>
      
      <para id="para-00005">Simplify:</para>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00001">
        <item>\(3(x+4)\)It may help to use arrows to show the Distributive Property.
    (<media alt=""><image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_07_02_06_01.jpg"/></media>)</item>
      </list>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00002">
        <item>\(-11(4-3a)\)
    Use arrows to show the Distributive Property. 
    (<media alt=""><image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_07_02_06_02.jpg"/></media>)</item>
      </list></section>

<note class="try" id="note-00002"><title>Try It: Simplify Expressions Using the Distributive Property </title>
        <exercise id="exer-00001">
          <problem id="prob-00001">
            <para id="para-00006">Simplify: \(-2(4y+1)\).</para>
            <para id="para-00007">Write down your answer. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00001">
            <para id="para-00008">Here is how to simplify expressions using the Distributive Property.</para>
            <para id="para-00009">Use arrows to show the Distributive Property.</para>
            <para id="para-00010">
                      <media alt="">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_07_02_06_03.jpg"/>
                </media>
              
          </para>
            <para id="para-00011">Use the Distributive Property.
    \(-2 \cdot 4y+(-2) \cdot 1\)</para>
            <para id="para-00012">Multiply.
    \(-8y+(-2)\)</para>
            <para id="para-00013">Simplify.
    \(-8y-2\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
</note>
      <section class="check-understanding" id="sect-00003">
        <title>Check Your Understanding</title>
        <exercise id="fe2725a9-e34e-4c6a-ba93-4368602af0de" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00002">
            <para id="para-00014">Which expression is equivalent to \(4(5-2a)\)?</para>
            <para id="para-00015">Multiple Choice:</para>
            <para id="para-00016">\(20-2a\)</para>
            <para id="para-00017">\(20+2a\)</para>
            <para id="para-00018">\(20-8a\)</para>
            <para id="para-00019">\(20+8a\)</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00002">
            <para id="para-00020">Correct! Check yourself: Multiply the factor outside the parentheses by each term inside the parentheses.</para>
            <para id="para-00021">Try again. Let’s try again a different way: To use the Distributive Property, multiply the factor outside the parentheses by each term inside the parentheses.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>
<section id="sect-00004">      <title>Videos </title>
      <para id="para-00022">Watch the first 2 minutes and 40 seconds of the video to see how to use the Distributive Property to simplify expressions.</para>
      <para id="para-00023">
       <emphasis>Khan Academy: Distributive property with variables</emphasis>
    </para>
